cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Code and aggregate results for "Refusal, Not Coarsening: What Enforcing
  k^m-Anonymity Costs on Longitudinal Patient Records, and Why Its Guarantee
  Resists Verification"
message: >-
  If you use this software or the aggregate results it contains, please cite
  both the software and the accompanying paper.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Pavlo
    family-names: Zamroz
    email: pavlo.i.zamroz@lpnu.ua
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0008-8722-5769'
    affiliation: >-
      Department of Electronic Computing Machines, Lviv Polytechnic National
      University, Lviv, Ukraine
license: MIT
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23318661
version: v1.0.1
repository-code: 'https://github.com/qupavlo/km-anonymity-clinical-trajectories'
abstract: >-
  Scripts and aggregate result files reproducing every number in a study of what
  enforcing k^m-anonymity costs on longitudinal patient records. The study
  measures the candidate-set distribution as a function of adversary knowledge,
  shows that a capped subset search is not a sound audit of the guarantee it
  certifies, and separates the downstream cost of generalization from the cost
  of refusing records that cannot be protected. Contains no patient data: the
  underlying MIMIC-IV database is distributed under a data use agreement that
  prohibits redistribution, and derived files carrying one row per patient are
  withheld.
keywords:
  - de-identification
  - re-identification risk
  - statistical disclosure control
  - k-anonymity
  - electronic health records
  - generalization
  - machine learning
